#X obj 60 40 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty tx81z.i~\ and\ tx81z~ 24 6 0 22 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 76 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Yamaha\ TX81Z\ emulation\ running\ the\ original\ firmware 24 6 0 16 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 120 tx81z.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty tx81z.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 tx81z.i~ is the TX81Z in one object: tx81z~ (the original firmware on an emulated CPU) \, the editor txedit \, the controller layer txcc \, MIDI in and out. Put it in a patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the editor \, see Editor. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1). The controller layer follows the receive channel tx81z~ reports. Page k of the controller is row k of the editor. \; It needs the firmware tx81z-v1.6.bin beside tx81z~ (inside tx81z.i~ the patch that holds tx81z~ is tx81z.i~.pd itself) \, see Firmware and battery RAM. \; tx81z~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: raw MIDI bytes from midiin go into its inlet \, its outlet 3 reports every change. Open tx81z.i~ to see the wiring. tx81zn.i~ is the same instrument with the native engine (no CPU emulation)., f 64;
#X obj 60 488 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 516 The editor is the window of tx81z.i~. Every change shows up in it (controller \, program change \, messages \, random). \; Rows of 8: rows 1 to 11 are the pages of the controller layer \, the last rows have no CC (mouse only) \, titled as in Controls. Each cell: name \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value as on the TX81Z. \; VOICE band: voice name \, bank and patch (program change I01-C32 \, in EDIT mode tx81z~ presses PLAY first \, the firmware ignores program changes while editing) \, MIDI ch (receive channel \, 0 = omni) \, the user bank (user N recalls a slot \, store to N chooses one \, save patch stores the voice there and writes the file \, load bank reads it again) \, factory init \, random voices. TX81Z LCD: the display of the firmware. \; random: a new voice of that kind into the edit buffer: pluck (harp \, piano \, guitar) \, bass \, lead \, brass \, pad \, strings \, total (anything that still sounds). Keep a good one with store to and save patch. \; All changes go to the edit buffer of the TX81Z. STORE on the TX81Z (press store) keeps a voice in bank I of the battery RAM., f 64;
#X obj 60 866 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 894 1 note \, cc \, pgm \, bend messages \, raw MIDI (float \, list \, \;   midi <bytes>) and the messages below., f 64;
#X obj 60 940 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 968 1 signal: left output (~55.9 kHz resampled). \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: lcd \, led \, info \, voice \, channel \, voicedata., f 64;
#X obj 60 1030 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets\ and\ user\ bank 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1058 The user bank (64 voices \, two TX81Z voice bulk dumps) is txuser-bank.syx in the folder of your patch \, so every project keeps its own. tx81z.i~ reads it at load if it is there \, otherwise the bank beside tx81z~ is the starting point. store and userwrite / userread without a file name use this file. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. tx81zn.i~ uses the same file. \; save is tx81z~'s own: it writes the battery RAM \, see Firmware and battery RAM., f 64;
#X obj 60 1216 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1244 note <p> <v> [ch] \, cc <n> <v> [ch] \, pgm <n> [ch] \, \;   bend <-8192..8191> [ch]: MIDI to the TX81Z \; <float> / list \, midi <bytes>: raw MIDI bytes ([midiin]) \; <name> <value>: a voice parameter \, TX81Z data value \, \;   e.g. alg 4 \, out1 99 (all names: Controls) \; n <name> <0-127>: the same from a pot / CC position \; ninfo <name>: outlet 3 gets info <name> <0-127> <value> \, \;   every change is reported this way by itself \; vced <p> <d> \, aced <p> <d>: raw parameter change \; dumpreq: ask the firmware for the edit buffer (also \;   done by itself after every program change) \; channel <0-16>: receive channel of the TX81Z \, 0 = omni. \;   note \, cc \, pgm \, bend without [ch] and SysEx follow it \; user <0-63>: recall a slot of the user bank \; userstep [n]: the user slot n after the one played last \; store [0-63]: the voice into a slot \, writes the user bank \; userread [file] \, userwrite [file]: the user bank \, see \;   Presets. A file name without a path is beside tx81z~. \; init: the TX81Z Init Voice \; getvoice: outlet 3 gets voicedata <name> <93 VCED> \;   <23 ACED> \, voicedata ...: that voice into the edit \;   buffer (bulk dump) \; random <kind> [seed]: a new voice into the edit buffer \, \;   kind: pluck bass lead brass pad strings total \; press <button> \, button <button> 0/1: front panel \; rom <file> \, nvram <file> \, save \, reset: firmware and \;   battery RAM (saved on save and when the patch closes) \; factoryinit: presets \, MIDI setup \, program change on \;   (runs by itself if the nvram file does not exist yet) \; pcswitch 0/1/2 \, sysparam <p> <d>: system setup \; gain <f>: output gain (default 1), f 64;
#X obj 60 1754 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1782 The original TX81Z firmware v1.6 runs on an emulated HD6303X CPU \, the YM2414 is ymfm's. A parameter change is sent to the firmware as SysEx (7 bytes \, about 2 ms at 31250 baud). tx81z~ sends only the newest value of each parameter and only while its MIDI input is idle \, so notes are never delayed by a turning knob. The steps themselves are the firmware's (e.g. 100 output levels): a fast sweep can sound stepped \, as on the real TX81Z., f 64;
#X obj 60 1924 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Firmware\ and\ battery\ RAM 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1952 Arguments: [tx81z~ <firmware> <battery RAM file>] \, paths relative to the patch that holds tx81z~ (for tx81z.i~: beside tx81z~ \, it gives tx81z-v1.6.bin tx81z.nvram). The firmware (64 KB \, e.g. tx81z-v1.6.bin from the MAME set tx81z) is not included. The battery RAM file keeps voices \, performances and the system setup. If it does not exist \, tx81z~ runs the factory init (about 26 s of DSP time \, it runs only while DSP is on): test mode loads the presets into the internal memory \, then the front panel sets MIDI Control: receive omni \, program change \, control change \, pitch bend and Exclusive on. Without Exclusive on the firmware ignores all SysEx \, so parameter changes and the editor would not work. Then it saves the file., f 64;
#X obj 60 2158 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2186 The voice parameters in the order of the editor: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range (TX81Z data value) \, CC number and what it does., f 64;
